# 3D Visualization System — Master Plan

**Phase:** 5 of frontend-v2 implementation plan
**Scope:** `apps/control-room/src/modules/viewport-3d/` and related kernel/domain infrastructure
**Status:** Draft — awaiting review

---

## 1. What Went Wrong in v1

The legacy viewport (`apps/web/src/features/viewport-unified/`, `features/view3d/`, `features/meshWorkspace/`) accumulated these architectural debts:

| Problem | Root cause | v2 fix |
|---|---|---|
| FDM and FEM rendered by separate code paths that were later merged | Two renderer implementations → frankensteined into one component tree with `discretization` prop forks | **One renderer, two adapters.** Renderer sees `Viewport3DRenderModel`, never `fdm`/`fem` strings. |
| Topology rebuild on quantity switch | No separation between topology geometry and field-value buffers | **Topology and field are separate layers** with independent revision tracking. |
| Continuous rendering even when idle | `requestAnimationFrame` loop without dirty gating | **Dirty-driven render loop.** Frame only on camera, resize, data, or explicit animation. |
| WebGL resource leaks on 3D↔2D switch | No resource tracker; disposal scattered across components | **`WebglResourceTracker`** — single registry with explicit `releaseAll()` on unmount. |
| Layer components as React wrappers (`ArrowLayer`, `MeshLayer`, etc.) | Mixing React component tree with imperative 3D rendering | **Layers are imperative renderer classes**, not React components. React owns toolbar/overlay only. |
| State coupling between viewport, inspector, and explorer | All shared one giant `ControlRoomContext` | **Kernel event bus + selection store.** Viewport emits `viewport:object-picked`, never imports inspector. |
| No multi-view support | Single fullscreen viewport; no split-screen capability | **`ViewportGrid`** — 1×1, 1×2, 2×2, 1+2 layouts like 3ds Max. |

## 2. Non-Negotiables (from specs and AGENTS.md)

1. One viewport tree for FDM and FEM — FDM/FEM differences live in domain adapters only.
2. Topology rebuilds separate from field-buffer swaps.
3. Dirty-driven rendering — zero frames when idle.
4. Every GPU resource has explicit ownership and disposal via `WebglResourceTracker`.
5. 2D viewports don't keep WebGL objects alive.
6. Renderer inputs are domain-neutral `Viewport3DRenderModel`, not API payloads.
7. Viewport modules can be disabled without breaking explorer, inspector, charts, or runtime commands.
8. No React state holding WebGL resources (geometries, materials, textures, typed arrays).

