## 4. Delivery Phases

### Phase 5a — Viewport Shell and Render Foundation
- Module manifest, store, `Viewport3DModule.tsx`
- `SceneController` with Three.js scene/camera/renderer init
- `DirtyRenderLoop` hook
- `WebglResourceTracker`
- Empty canvas with camera orbit and resize

### Phase 5b — Domain Adapters and Topology
- Port `SpatialDomainAdapter` interface to `src/domain/`
- Implement FDM and FEM adapters
- `MeshLayerRenderer` — renders topology geometry from `MeshRenderModel`
- Wire resource hooks for mesh topology and coordinates

### Phase 5c — Field Visualization
- `FieldSurfaceRenderer` — scalar color mapping on topology
- `VectorGlyphRenderer` — sampled arrow/cone glyphs
- Field resource hooks and buffer management
- Quantity switching without topology rebuild

### Phase 5d — Multi-Viewport Grid
- `ViewportGrid` component and layout store
- Per-pane `ViewportPane` with independent camera
- Display mode per pane (shaded, wireframe, wireframe+shaded, flat)
- Camera linking / unlinking
- Keyboard shortcuts for layout switching

### Phase 5e — Selection, Picking, Overlays
- `SelectionLayerRenderer` — highlight selected objects
- `useViewportPicking` — GPU or raycasting pick
- `AxesGridRenderer`, `AirboxRenderer`
- `ViewportToolbar` and `ViewportOverlayStack`
- Integration with kernel `SelectionController`

### Phase 5f — Polish and Verification
- Idle performance audit
- Memory stress test (3D↔2D, quantity switch cycles)
- Context loss recovery
- Diagnostics surface for resource counts and dirty reasons

## 5. Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Multi-viewport camera linking behavior:**
> In 3ds Max, the 4 viewports are typically Front/Top/Right/Perspective and are independent.
> Do you want independent cameras by default with optional linking?
> Or should one "master" perspective control the others?

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Gizmo/transform tool:**
> ADR-0007 defines camera vs manipulate modes. For the initial v2 viewport, do you want transform gizmos (move/rotate/scale) in Phase 5, or defer to Phase 4 geometry-authoring?

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Render backend preference:**
> The spec says Three.js. Are you happy with Three.js, or would you prefer a lower-level approach (raw WebGL2/WebGPU)? Three.js gives faster iteration; raw WebGL gives more control and smaller bundle.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Display modes per viewport pane:**
> Proposed set: `shaded`, `wireframe`, `shaded+wireframe`, `flat-shaded`, `points`.
> Want anything else? (e.g., `xray`, `normals-debug`, `quality-heatmap`)
